{"name":"Admiral Blue","hex":"#50647f","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"admiral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The commander of a naval squadron or fleet, regardless of formal rank. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The appointed commander of a navy, regardless of formal title."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/admiral"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Admiral","blocks":[{"text":"Admiral is one of the highest ranks in many navies. In the Commonwealth nations and the United States, a \"full\" admiral is equivalent to a \"full\" general or air chief marshal in the army or the air force. Admiral is ranked above vice admiral and below admiral of the fleet, or fleet admiral."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Admiral","wikidata":"Q132851"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawar